      "title": "30.7.1 Chemical Environment of Carbon",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "2d720c54-503b-58ec-b5b1-3307f222f40c",
          "pdf_from": 212,
          "pdf_to": 213,
          "note": "Topic 30.7.1 with Table 30.3 (PDF 212-213)."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "A carbon atom's environment is decided by the types of atoms directly attached to it. Carbon atoms in the same environment are chemically equivalent and have the same chemical shift: both carbons of ethane are equivalent. Carbon atoms in different environments are non-equivalent and appear at different chemical shifts: in 2-methylpropane, (CH3)3CH, the three methyl carbons share one environment while the central carbon has a different one. The type of environment can be predicted from the atoms or bonds around each carbon."
          },
